      "text": "To determine the values of \\( y \\) for which the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined, we need to identify when the denominator is equal to zero. The expression is undefined when the denominator is zero because division by zero is not defined in mathematics.\n\nThe denominator of the expression is \\( y^2 - 5y + 4 \\). We need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[ y^2 - 5y + 4 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, and we can solve it by factoring. We look for two numbers that multiply to \\( 4 \\) (the constant term) and add up to \\( -5 \\) (the coefficient of the linear term). These numbers are \\( -1 \\) and \\( -4 \\). Therefore, we can factor the quadratic expression as follows:\n\n\\[ y^2 - 5y + 4 = (y - 1)(y - 4) \\]\n\nSetting the factored form equal to zero gives us:\n\n\\[ (y - 1)(y - 4) = 0 \\]\n\nThis equation will be satisfied when either factor is zero. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[ y - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y - 4 = 0 \\]\n\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\n\\[ y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = 4 \\]\n\nThe expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined for \\( y = 1 \\) and \\( y = 4 \\). To find the sum of all such values of \\( y \\), we add these solutions together:\n\n\\[ 1 + 4 = 5 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all values of \\( y \\) for which the expression is undefined is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
